te and stated base are shown. |\n\n## Questions to ask\n1. “What inspection findings led you to quote the front wheel bearing replacement?” — A good answer points to specific findings from the inspection, rather than just repeating the symptom.\n2. “Is the $145.00 diagnostic scan included in the total, and how would the credit apply if I approve the repair?” — A good answer explains whether the credit is already reflected in this total or would change the amount due.\n3. “Could you walk me through the $41.13 sales-tax calculation on parts and labor?” — A good answer shows how the stated 7% rate and base produce the amount shown. The listed parts and labor total $430.00; 7% of that is $30.10, so the stated tax amount needs explaining.\n4. “Can you confirm whether the estimate’s 30-day validity period runs from the date shown?” — A good answer confirms the applicable end date.\n\n## Decision\nThe questions are for understanding the estimate; you decide what to do. Photograph the estimate, note what was promised verbally and by whom, and keep the paperwork chain.\n\n## Needs confirmation\n[CONFIRM: How the $41.13 tax amount was calculated from the stated 7% rate on parts and labor.]  \n[CONFIRM: Whether the diagnostic credit is already reflected in the total or would be applied later.]  \n[NOT SHOWN: Quantity for the wheel bearing.]","refusal":null,"reasoning":"**Questioning tax calculations**\n\nI'm noticing that the printed total adds lines, but the tax rate doesn't align with the declared base. It seems there might be a calculation inconsistency with the tax basis. The tax line at the stated rate is always labeled as Standard, which raises questions about the math behind the tax base. Specifically, Tax line 41.13 doesn’t seem to equal the expected parts and labor amount. I should confirm this discrepancy further to ensure clarity.**Analyzing estimate components**\n\nThe user has provided a symptom that suggests a bearing replacement due to a front humming noise whil